\title{fastmsna: fast, survey-correct descriptive analysis for MSNA datasets}
\description{
\code{fastmsna} re-implements the calculation layer of \code{analysistools}
(\code{create_analysis()} and the \verb{create_analysis_*()} family) for large
datasets. Results (estimates, confidence intervals, \code{n}, \code{n_total}, \code{n_w},
\code{n_w_total}, analysis keys) follow the same methodology as
\code{analysistools}/\code{srvyr}/\code{survey}:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item weighted means, proportions and ratios with Taylor-linearised variances
for stratified (and optionally clustered, with fpc) designs;
\item domain (subgroup) estimation that keeps the full-design number of PSUs
per stratum, exactly like \code{survey};
\item \code{survey.lonely.psu} / \code{survey.adjust.domain.lonely} handling;
\item weighted medians with the "school" quantile rule and Woodruff
confidence intervals.
}

The speed-up comes from preparing the design once, computing grouping
indexes once per disaggregation, and computing every group of every
indicator in a handful of vectorised \code{rowsum()} passes, instead of building
one survey-design subset (with a full copy of the dataset) per group and per
indicator.

Main entry points: \code{\link[=create_analysis_fast]{create_analysis_fast()}}, \code{\link[=run_disaggregated_analysis_fast]{run_disaggregated_analysis_fast()}},
\code{\link[=as_fast_design]{as_fast_design()}}, \code{\link[=compare_analysis_results]{compare_analysis_results()}}.
